## Tracks

Six parallel lines of work, one per physical thing that has to keep moving on its own clock:

- **pork** — the shoulder's own timeline as it moves smoker → wrap → smoker → cooler → hands: `light-smoker → smoke-unwrapped → wrap-shoulder → smoke-wrapped → hold-cooler → pull-pork`.
- **trim** — the only step that runs *while* the smoker is heating, so it can't share the pork track: `trim-rub-shoulder`.
- **beans** — side-burner work, gated on the pork going into the cooler: `light-burner → fry-bacon-onion → simmer-beans`.
- **slaw** — same start signal as beans, different resources: `shred-cabbage-carrot → whisk-dressing → dress-slaw → refrigerate-slaw`.
- **grill** — the kettle line, timed backward from serving: `light-kettle → grill-sausages → grill-corn → toast-buns`.
- **table** — the closing line: `set-table → call-everyone-serve`.

## Triggers

- `trim-rub-shoulder`: `programStart` (parallel to `light-smoker`).
- `smoke-unwrapped`: `compound/all` of `afterStep light-smoker` and `afterStep trim-rub-shoulder` — the shoulder can't go on until the smoker holds temp *and* it's trimmed and rubbed; trim finishes first (25 m < 45 m) so the smoker is the actual gate.
- `wrap-shoulder`, `smoke-wrapped`, `hold-cooler`, `pull-pork`: each `afterStep` the previous pork step.
- `light-burner` and `shred-cabbage-carrot`: `afterStep hold-cooler` with `"event":"start"` — both fire "when the pork goes into the cooler," not when it comes out.
- `fry-bacon-onion` afterStep `light-burner`; `simmer-beans` afterStep `fry-bacon-onion`.
- `whisk-dressing`, `dress-slaw`, `refrigerate-slaw`: each `afterStep` the previous slaw step.
- `light-kettle`: `programStartOffset` timed so the grill line lands its last step (`toast-buns`) right when the pork is ready — computed below.
- `grill-sausages`, `grill-corn`, `toast-buns`: each `afterStep` the previous grill step.
- `set-table`: `afterStep toast-buns` ("once the buns are off").
- `call-everyone-serve`: `compound/all` of `afterStep set-table`, `afterStep pull-pork`, `afterStep refrigerate-slaw` — the earliest instant every dish is actually ready; "both cooks say it's ready" is the human read of exactly this moment, so no separate `manual` gate is needed to find the earliest time.

## Refining the question

"Which activities depend on which, and which can run at the same time?" hides the fact that some things must be *heated before use* and some things must *converge at one instant*, neither of which is a simple predecessor relationship. Better question: **"Besides direct hand-offs, which activities require a resource to already be up to working temperature before they can start, and which activities must all land at the same clock moment for service to work?"**

Answering it: the smoker and the kettle both get explicit "light it first" steps in the text — the side burner does not, but a cold side burner won't fry bacon any better than a cold smoker will hold temperature. That's the gap: an inferred `light-burner` step (5 min) belongs before `fry-bacon-onion`, on the `burner` task, triggered the same way beans start (`afterStep hold-cooler, event:start`). The convergence half of the question is answered by `call-everyone-serve`'s compound trigger: pulled pork, dressed/chilled slaw, and a set table must all be true at once, and working backward from the 46,500 s pork-ready time is what fixes `light-kettle`'s `programStartOffset` (42,780 s) so the buns come off right as the pork comes off hold — no dish waits on another.

Makespan check by hand: pork chain (using default variable durations) — light-smoker 2700s → smoke-unwrapped ends 24300s → wrap-shoulder ends 24900s → smoke-wrapped (default 12600s) ends 37500s → hold-cooler (default 7200s) ends 44700s → pull-pork ends **46500s**. Slaw finishes at 42180s, well inside. Grill+table (3720s + 600s = 4320s) started at 42780s finishes set-table at 46500s exactly. So `call-everyone-serve` starts at 46500s — the pork is the binding constraint, and nothing else is made to wait longer than it already must.

